WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.238
This is London's financial center,

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.863
the City of London, at 1pm

00:00:07.863 --> 00:00:10.905
Around half a million people work here.

00:00:10.905 --> 00:00:13.071
Well, not any more.

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:16.488
The Covid-19 pandemic has
changed our relationship

00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:19.155
with the office perhaps forever.

00:00:19.155 --> 00:00:21.363
Millions of white-collar workers have seen

00:00:21.363 --> 00:00:24.030
they don't have to be married
to a regimented

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.905
commute-to-work, at-your-desk-by-nine system.

00:00:26.905 --> 00:00:28.905
Instead they have found they can have

00:00:28.905 --> 00:00:31.613
a more open relationship with the office.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.321
But the effects of that
change are significant,

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:37.155
streets like this which at rush hour

00:00:37.155 --> 00:00:40.446
you could barely squeeze
down are now quiet

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:43.613
and inside these towers
it's the same situation.

00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:47.530
Although after the pandemic,
things will get busier,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.113
it's not clear they will
return to the way they were.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:54.571
Survey after survey shows
workers want flexibility

00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:57.030
but more surprisingly, managers and bosses

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.488
are listening and many
are joining the push

00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:01.488
for flexible work.

00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:04.196
So are these changes here to stay

00:01:04.196 --> 00:01:07.196
and if they are, what does
that mean for the office

00:01:07.196 --> 00:01:09.071
and those of us who work in them?

00:01:16.488 --> 00:01:19.738
During the Covid lockdown,
there's been a schism

00:01:19.738 --> 00:01:22.321
between those who've realized - wait,

00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:25.738
I need the office - like me
and those who are happy

00:01:25.738 --> 00:01:29.655
to spend less time there
like my co-producer Tom.

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:30.738
When I take a coffee break,

00:01:30.738 --> 00:01:32.655
I get to see my three-year-old

00:01:32.655 --> 00:01:34.196
and that is great.

00:01:34.196 --> 00:01:35.738
I've enjoyed that a lot.

00:01:35.738 --> 00:01:38.738
And I'm also kind of fortunate,

00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:42.363
I have a good microphone here,
I have a camera from work,

00:01:42.363 --> 00:01:46.738
I can move it around
and hide my mess in time

00:01:46.738 --> 00:01:47.571
to do an interview.

00:01:47.571 --> 00:01:51.988
Like it has worked pretty well for me

00:01:51.988 --> 00:01:56.571
to do my job and do it in this
sort of isolated workstation

00:01:56.571 --> 00:01:57.405
that I've created.

00:01:57.405 --> 00:02:00.905
I live in a shared flat where it's hard

00:02:00.905 --> 00:02:04.446
to be productive and given the choice

00:02:04.446 --> 00:02:07.988
between an office with
a desk and free coffee

00:02:07.988 --> 00:02:10.571
and all the rest, it's a simple choice.

00:02:10.571 --> 00:02:12.780
I like being in the office.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.113
It just has to be different
for different people, right?

00:02:15.113 --> 00:02:16.946
Different people have different struggles

00:02:16.946 --> 00:02:20.655
and different people find
the office as a solution

00:02:20.655 --> 00:02:23.113
or find the office as a
part of that struggle.

00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:28.530
The Covid pandemic has forced

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.780
a radical experiment on us.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.696
And the results of that
experiment are coming in.

00:02:33.696 --> 00:02:37.405
Companies now know if
employees can work remotely,

00:02:37.405 --> 00:02:40.655
they know if teams are
still being productive

00:02:40.655 --> 00:02:43.988
and workers have gotten
a taste for flexibility

00:02:43.988 --> 00:02:45.071
and they like it.

00:02:46.071 --> 00:02:48.821
Before the paradigm in
the office world was,

00:02:48.821 --> 00:02:51.113
I'm going to the office nine-to-five

00:02:51.113 --> 00:02:52.696
and if not, I have to explain why

00:02:52.696 --> 00:02:54.238
or I'm doing something unusual.

00:02:54.238 --> 00:02:57.196
Now, I know that I can stay
home one or two days a week

00:02:57.196 --> 00:02:58.655
or that I could come at different hours

00:02:58.655 --> 00:03:00.196
and that impacts overall demand.

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:04.030
Dror Poleg is an author
and property advisor

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.571
to some of the world's biggest companies.

00:03:06.571 --> 00:03:09.196
He argues things aren't going back

00:03:09.196 --> 00:03:11.905
to the way they were - deal with it.

00:03:11.905 --> 00:03:14.780
I think at some point things
change fundamentally.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.280
There's a difference
between employees being away

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.655
or offices being empty
for two or three months

00:03:19.655 --> 00:03:22.196
and between offices being
empty for a year or 15 months

00:03:22.196 --> 00:03:24.196
like, you just don't go
back to the same place

00:03:24.196 --> 00:03:26.238
after such a length of period.

00:03:31.905 --> 00:03:34.530
Dror argues workers have adjusted,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.321
they've got new routines and habits.

00:03:37.321 --> 00:03:40.321
So what will become the new normal?

00:03:40.321 --> 00:03:42.780
Surveys show workers
are splitting their time

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.946
between the office and home
and want to keep it that way.

00:03:46.946 --> 00:03:50.030
Management liked the idea too.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.196
A UK survey of over 2,000
office workers found most

00:03:54.196 --> 00:03:57.946
do not intend to spend five
days a week in the workplace,

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:01.738
even when the pandemic
is over. With both bosses

00:04:01.738 --> 00:04:05.738
and employees seeing homeworking
as a long-term trend.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.863
A survey by business consultancy McKenzie

00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:14.863
of 800 global executives
found 15% will allow staff

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:18.488
to work remotely two or more days a week.

00:04:18.488 --> 00:04:21.696
In the finance sector, the
Netherlands' biggest bank predicts

00:04:21.696 --> 00:04:25.238
that staff will be working
about half their time from home

00:04:25.238 --> 00:04:27.571
once the pandemic is over.

00:04:27.571 --> 00:04:30.821
And in Italy Uni Credit plans to have 40%

00:04:30.821 --> 00:04:34.321
of work done remotely after the crisis.

00:04:34.321 --> 00:04:36.280
It's important to say this shift

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.030
is not being seen across the economy.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.030
In the U.S. more than 60% of
workers cannot work remotely,

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.571
their jobs require at least
some physical presence,

00:04:46.571 --> 00:04:49.071
but for millions of white collar workers

00:04:49.071 --> 00:04:51.613
the scale and speed of this change

00:04:51.613 --> 00:04:53.530
should not be underestimated.

00:04:54.821 --> 00:04:56.946
In a prime market like New York or London

00:04:56.946 --> 00:04:59.655
or San Francisco or even
Shanghai or Hong Kong,

00:04:59.655 --> 00:05:03.280
a company spends between
12,000 to $20,000 a year

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.363
on putting an employee
in a seat, in an office.

00:05:06.363 --> 00:05:08.238
Now, one of the most interesting questions

00:05:08.238 --> 00:05:11.530
that I think companies will
ask themselves now is not - okay,

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.113
can I save money by
telling everyone to go home - 

00:05:15.113 --> 00:05:19.238
but they can ask themselves
if I take $15,000

00:05:19.238 --> 00:05:20.696
and spend it differently

00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:22.696
can I make my employee more productive?

00:05:22.696 --> 00:05:24.613
Can I make my employee happier?

00:05:24.613 --> 00:05:27.571
Can I maybe attract better
people than I currently have?

00:05:27.571 --> 00:05:28.780
And I think in many cases

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.780
the answer to that question will be yes.

00:05:34.446 --> 00:05:35.780
Dror believes we are living

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.280
through nothing short of a revolution

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.196
in the way we use offices.

00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:43.821
And there are going to
be winners and losers.

00:05:43.821 --> 00:05:47.363
To destabilize the office and
to cause a revolution,

00:05:47.363 --> 00:05:50.821
you only need 10 to 15% of demand to shift

00:05:50.821 --> 00:05:52.821
and I think that's what's happening.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.530
Jack, there's been recent survey data

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.613
that you've got hold of about
occupancy rates around here.

00:05:59.613 --> 00:06:00.446
What are they like?

00:06:00.446 --> 00:06:02.030
Are these buildings practically empty?

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.488
Yeah, I mean, they're certainly
more than half empty.

00:06:04.488 --> 00:06:05.613
Jack Sidders is

00:06:05.613 --> 00:06:07.363
Bloomberg's European
commercial property reporter

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:10.280
and he's been told privately

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.321
what commercial real estate
agencies won't say publicly.

00:06:14.321 --> 00:06:17.155
If you were speaking to
them off the record

00:06:17.155 --> 00:06:21.946
then you'd detect a much more
significant amount of concern.

00:06:21.946 --> 00:06:22.946
And you actually you see it

00:06:22.946 --> 00:06:24.113
with some of the public statements,

00:06:24.113 --> 00:06:27.196
you've seen real estate leaders
come out and say publicly,

00:06:27.196 --> 00:06:29.863
look, we need to get people back, come on,

00:06:29.863 --> 00:06:32.155
come on politicians you
need to sort this out.

00:06:34.571 --> 00:06:35.488
So those of you

00:06:35.488 --> 00:06:37.321
who are feeling very comfortable,

00:06:37.321 --> 00:06:39.446
never coming back into the office,

00:06:39.446 --> 00:06:43.196
well bad news, experts
agree that for most of us

00:06:43.196 --> 00:06:45.738
we will still have a
relationship with the office

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.280
just not as demanding

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.571
but even if workers return
four out of five work days,

00:06:52.571 --> 00:06:54.655
that's still a huge reduction

00:06:54.655 --> 00:06:57.196
and it's got investor's attention.

00:06:57.196 --> 00:06:59.280
What you see if you look at share prices

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.696
of the big office landlords is that

00:07:02.696 --> 00:07:05.488
they're down substantially this year.

00:07:05.488 --> 00:07:07.988
Much of the London, the big
London office landlords,

00:07:07.988 --> 00:07:11.071
are down 30, 40, 50% this year

00:07:11.071 --> 00:07:14.571
but the valuation of their
assets has barely changed.

00:07:14.571 --> 00:07:17.738
It's likely that both of
those numbers are wrong

00:07:17.738 --> 00:07:20.530
and it'll be somewhere in between.

00:07:24.488 --> 00:07:27.113
With growing consensus that office workers

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:29.571
will be splitting their
time between the office

00:07:29.571 --> 00:07:31.613
and perhaps their home,

00:07:31.613 --> 00:07:34.863
flexible workspaces find
themselves well-positioned

00:07:34.863 --> 00:07:36.363
to take advantage.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:41.571
Tania Adir is the co-founder
and CEO of Uncommon,

00:07:41.571 --> 00:07:46.571
a London-based flexible working
company founded in 2015.

00:07:46.613 --> 00:07:50.280
She agrees things aren't going
back to the way they were

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.405
but sees a bright future for the office.

00:07:53.405 --> 00:07:55.238
It's very difficult to run a team

00:07:55.238 --> 00:07:57.155
if people are completely in
different locations.

00:07:57.155 --> 00:07:58.030
Yes you can work from home,

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.530
you can work from a
coffee shop one day a week

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:03.488
but at the same time to
really have that connection

00:08:03.488 --> 00:08:06.863
between individuals where
I think ideas come to life,

00:08:06.863 --> 00:08:09.613
you do want to be present
in physical space together.

00:08:09.613 --> 00:08:12.405
Tania is betting offices will shift

00:08:12.405 --> 00:08:15.946
from being primarily places
where you go to get work done

00:08:15.946 --> 00:08:20.238
to places where you go to
collaborate and build teams.

00:08:20.238 --> 00:08:21.321
You don't necessarily
need to go to the office,

00:08:21.321 --> 00:08:24.113
you don't need to waste
the time of commuting.

00:08:24.113 --> 00:08:26.655
The reasons for that office to exist

00:08:26.655 --> 00:08:28.530
are fundamentally to come and meet

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.696
your colleagues, collaborate,

00:08:30.696 --> 00:08:33.905
be able to come together
and work on the presentation

00:08:33.905 --> 00:08:38.905
on the new idea to exchange
sort of energies fundamentally

00:08:38.905 --> 00:08:39.738
with each other.

00:08:41.238 --> 00:08:43.530
This trend of making offices

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:47.363
places for collaboration
has been going on for years

00:08:47.363 --> 00:08:50.488
but the Covid pandemic
has shifted the dial.

00:08:50.488 --> 00:08:53.905
The argument's not around
will work be more flexible,

00:08:53.905 --> 00:08:56.488
it's about how flexible it will be.

00:08:57.488 --> 00:08:59.655
The office of the future is not a place.

00:08:59.655 --> 00:09:01.821
The office of the future
is a network that allows me

00:09:01.821 --> 00:09:04.488
to enable my employees
to access whatever it is

00:09:04.488 --> 00:09:06.030
that they need to access right now

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.821
in order to produce
their best possible work.

00:09:07.821 --> 00:09:10.071
And of course to be happy
and to keep coming back

00:09:10.071 --> 00:09:11.863
and to give their talents to my business

00:09:11.863 --> 00:09:13.696
and not to anyone else.

00:09:13.696 --> 00:09:16.196
Tania agrees that satellite offices

00:09:16.196 --> 00:09:19.821
will become more common
but argues for most of us,

00:09:19.821 --> 00:09:22.738
the office will still play a major role.

00:09:22.738 --> 00:09:24.613
Naturally when everything normalizes,

00:09:24.613 --> 00:09:28.363
people would want to still
have their headquarters,

00:09:28.363 --> 00:09:32.113
their culture, their identity
to be able to connect

00:09:32.113 --> 00:09:34.821
and I think there is a very loud voice

00:09:34.821 --> 00:09:37.738
out there saying we're all
very productive from home

00:09:37.738 --> 00:09:39.405
but I think there is a silent majority

00:09:39.405 --> 00:09:41.113
that are not that productive at home.

00:09:41.113 --> 00:09:43.571
And they just, they are not being necessarily being heard 

00:09:43.571 --> 00:09:46.238
because your kids are around,
your pet is disturbing you,

00:09:46.238 --> 00:09:50.405
your neighbors doing decoration, renovation

00:09:50.405 --> 00:09:51.863
and making noise.

00:09:56.863 --> 00:09:58.988
After a year of working from home,

00:09:58.988 --> 00:10:01.821
the gloss may have come off
and many of us will start

00:10:01.821 --> 00:10:06.655
to miss the office.. But will
anyone, bosses or workers,

00:10:06.655 --> 00:10:10.071
really want to go back to the old ways?

00:10:10.071 --> 00:10:13.238
v

00:10:13.238 --> 00:10:15.613
but instead of cramming
us in the new office

00:10:15.613 --> 00:10:19.155
may well be spacier with
more room to collaborate.

00:10:19.155 --> 00:10:22.363
And most importantly, we
will come in when we need to,

00:10:22.363 --> 00:10:23.738
not because we have to.

